<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinterklaas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sint-Nicolaas, De Sint, De Goede Sint, De Goedheiligman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sinterklaas is a legendary figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> tradition based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a> (270–343), a Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myra">Myra</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and patron saint of children. He is depicted as an elderly, stately man with a long beard, in a red cape and mitre, who traditionally arrives by steamboat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in mid-November and rides a white horse. Gifts are given on St. Nicholas' Eve, 5 December, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and on the morning of 6 December in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, Luxembourg, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His helper Zwarte Piet (&quot;Black Pete&quot;), who first appeared in print in 1850, has been the subject of debate and protest since the 2010s over his blackface appearance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sinterklaas.jpg" alt="Sinterklaas" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R.F. (Roel) Jorna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amerigo_with_Sinterklaas_2008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sinterklaas</b>, the Dutch figure based on him, traditionally arrives by steamboat from Spain in mid-November; gifts are given on 5 December in the Netherlands and on the morning of 6 December in Belgium, Luxembourg, western Germany and northern France.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
